# Chapter 10: Introduction to LLM Agents & LangGraph

The shift from chains to agents is a shift from ﬁxed pipelines to systems
that can decide what step to take next.
  
Agents introduce more moving parts than chains:

	state
    tool calls
	loops
	branching decisions
	failure handling
	multiple cooperating actors.
	
An agent has a goal, observes the current situation, chooses an action,
sees the result, and decides what to do next.

	autonomy
	adaptive reasoning
	language understanding
	workflow optimization
	multi-agent & system interaction

LangChain’s founder, **Harrison Chase**, has described every agent as resting on
four building blocks: Planning, Memory, Tools, and Action. They run in a loop
until the task is done. This loop is the basic shape behind many agent systems,
whether the implementation is a few lines of Python or a production LangGraph
application.
